  phases: [
    { title: 'Implement', detail: 'one worktree per lane; tests first, scoped cargo only' },
    { title: 'Review', detail: 'independent adversarial review of each implementation' },
    { title: 'Reproduce', detail: 'W9a: status vs version resolver divergence' },
  ],
}

const BASE = '/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/wave1-base'

const RULES = `You are working on the Callisto repository (Rust workspace: callisto-model MIT, callisto-manifests, callisto-graph, callisto-cli). You were started in your own git worktree, created from current main. Work only inside it; never edit ${BASE} or any other path.

HARD CONSTRAINTS
- git commands are refused in this sandbox. Do NOT try to commit, branch, push or open PRs: leave your change uncommitted. Use ordinary file tools and cargo.
- Do NOT run \`just ci\` or \`just coverage\` or any whole-workspace test: the coordinator runs the full gate serially. Run only scoped checks for the crate(s) you touch: \`cargo test -p <crate> ...\`, \`cargo clippy -p <crate> --all-targets -- -D warnings\`, \`cargo fmt -p <crate>\`. Run one cargo command at a time. Use \`env -u FORCE_COLOR\` if a test about colors fails.
- Repository rules (AGENTS.md): safe Rust only; TOML edits go through toml_edit and JSON edits preserve key order and indentation, never regex or line edits; file writes go through callisto_model::atomic::atomic_write; user-facing errors derive miette::Diagnostic with a code and a help line; no emojis in docs or code; code comments are one short line and only for a non-obvious why, never history ("previously", "used to"); never cite spec IDs, criterion IDs or section numbers in code, comments, test names or messages; no cargo features.
- Write the failing test FIRST, run it, and confirm it fails for the right reason before writing the fix. Then fix, then run the tests again. Record the failing output in your report. Test adversarial inputs, not only the case being fixed.
- If you cannot reproduce the problem as described, STOP and report what you found instead; do not invent a fix.
- Be precise in your report. Never claim a command passed unless you ran it and saw it pass; list anything you could not run.`

const IMPL_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    worktree: { type: 'string', description: 'absolute path of your worktree' },
    stopped_early: { type: 'boolean' },
    stop_reason: { type: 'string' },
    summary: { type: 'string' },
    files_changed: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    tests_added: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          name: { type: 'string' },
          file: { type: 'string' },
          failed_before_fix: { type: 'boolean' },
          failure_evidence: { type: 'string' },
        },
        required: ['name', 'file', 'failed_before_fix'],
      },
    },
    commands_run: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: { command: { type: 'string' }, result: { type: 'string' } },
        required: ['command', 'result'],
      },
    },
    not_run: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    design_notes: { type: 'string' },
    public_api_changes: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    risks: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
  },
  required: ['worktree', 'stopped_early', 'summary', 'files_changed', 'tests_added', 'commands_run', 'not_run'],
}

const REVIEW_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    verdict: { type: 'string', enum: ['approve', 'changes-needed', 'reject'] },
    findings: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['high', 'medium', 'low'] },
          where: { type: 'string', description: 'file:line' },
          scenario: { type: 'string', description: 'concrete inputs and state, then the wrong behavior' },
          confirmed_by: { type: 'string', enum: ['ran-a-test', 'read-the-code'] },
        },
        required: ['severity', 'where', 'scenario', 'confirmed_by'],
      },
    },
    tests_run: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    not_checked: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
  },
  required: ['verdict', 'findings', 'tests_run', 'not_checked'],
}

const W10_PROMPT = `${RULES}

TASK (W10: track distinct Cargo dependency declarations).
Verified current behavior (confirm it yourself first, do not trust this blindly): crates/callisto-manifests/src/cargo.rs, in iter_dependencies (around line 376), reports one edge per (dependency kind, name) across [dependencies] and every [target.<cfg>.dependencies] table: the FIRST declaration is reported and a later declaration of the same key is skipped (see the comment there and the "reported" set). Rewrites reach only declarations identical to the reported one. So when a crate declares the same dependency twice with DIFFERENT constraints (for example in [dependencies] as "^1.0.0" and in [target.'cfg(unix)'.dependencies] as "=1.0.0" or ">=1.0, <2"), a version bump of that dependency rewrites at most one of them, and the other keeps a stale constraint that can break the workspace or publish stale metadata.

Steps
1. Reproduce black-box with the real binary. Model it on crates/callisto-cli/tests/cascade_scenarios_tests.rs and the existing Cargo target-table tests (search for "target" in crates/callisto-cli/tests and crates/callisto-graph/tests). Build a fixture workspace with crate b (a dependency) and crate a that declares b twice with different constraints as above, add a changeset bumping b, run the real \`callisto version\` and assert the exact bytes of a's Cargo.toml. Show that the second declaration is left stale. Also reproduce at the unit level (extraction reports one entry; the writer touches one).
2. Fix it. The design needs a declaration locator (which table: the section kind and the optional target cfg key) carried from extraction through DependencyEntry / DepEdge (crates/callisto-model/src/dependency.rs), the graph's edge construction (crates/callisto-graph/src/walk.rs build_edges), RewriteKey (crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs), the snapshot rewriting path, apply (crates/callisto-graph/src/apply.rs rewrite_dependency) and the format-preserving Cargo writer in cargo.rs (update_dependency_spec), so each distinct declaration is its own edge and is rewritten independently while preserving formatting and comments. Read those files first and keep the change as small as the design allows. The Manifest trait is implemented for npm and Python too: keep their behavior unchanged and give them the minimal locator value. Declarations that are byte-identical across tables may stay one edge only if you can show a rewrite still reaches all of them; otherwise make them separate edges.
3. Tests, in addition to the black-box one: two same-name declarations with different constraints, one already current and one stale (only the stale one is rewritten); target table plus plain table; a renamed dependency (package = "...") declared in both; an inherited workspace = true declaration alongside a target one; dev and build kinds; format and comment preservation of the untouched declaration; snapshot and floor rewriting paths if they share the key type.
4. Public API: callisto-model and callisto-graph are published crates. Record every public signature or type you change in public_api_changes, and run \`just check-api\` if it works in this sandbox (it is a compatibility check, not a substitute for migrating constructors). Update every constructor and call site, including the fixtures crate (crates/callisto-fixtures) and tests.
5. Scoped verification only: cargo test and clippy for callisto-model, callisto-manifests, callisto-graph and callisto-cli, one at a time. Add a changeset note text suggestion in your report (the coordinator creates the file): one sentence a user would read, no internal names.
Do not edit docs/projects/*, ARCHITECTURE.md or specs; report which spec or architecture sentences would need to change.`

const W9B_PROMPT = `${RULES}

TASK (W9b: a promoted pre-state key must not silently drop a version).
Verified site: crates/callisto-graph/src/lib.rs, function assign_promoted_pre_keys (around line 281; the merge is near line 336): when a legacy bare key in the pre-release state resolves to an owner that already has a canonical key, it does shift_remove(&key) on the legacy key and then entry(owner_key).or_insert(version), so the canonical value silently wins and the migrated legacy value is dropped. (Confirm by reading.) The pre-state file is pre.json, type PreState in crates/callisto-model/src/format/pre.rs.

Required behavior
- Compare BEFORE mutating anything. If the legacy key and the canonical owner key carry different versions, fail with a new typed error naming BOTH keys and BOTH versions, and leave the pre-state unmodified. If they carry equal versions (compare as parsed versions in the owning package's grammar, not as raw strings; state in your report what you chose for different spellings of an equal version), coalesce as today.
- The error is a new GraphError variant in crates/callisto-graph/src/error.rs deriving miette::Diagnostic with a code and a help line that tells the user how to fix pre.json. Use the next unused E-code: grep both the code and docs/errors.md for the highest code (docs show E304 as the highest, verify against the source) and add the row to docs/errors.md in the same style as its neighbours.
- Find every caller of assign_promoted_pre_keys and make sure the error surfaces through the real commands (callisto pre ..., callisto version, callisto status) with a readable message and a non-zero exit, with nothing written.
- Do not change which owner an alias resolves to; that belongs to a separate historical-owner change. If you notice the owner resolution can pick the wrong owner, report it, do not fix it.

Tests (first, failing): unit tests in crates/callisto-graph (differing versions: error names both keys and versions and the state is untouched; equal versions coalesce; legacy key with no canonical key migrates; three-way cases if reachable; prerelease versions and a Python PEP 440 package), plus one black-box test with the real binary through the CLI (see crates/callisto-cli/tests/identity_promotion_tests.rs and pre_mode_tests.rs for fixtures that promote a bare name to ecosystem-qualified keys). Verify the black-box test fails before the fix because the legacy version is dropped.
Scoped verification only. Suggest a one-sentence changeset text a user would read. Do not edit docs other than docs/errors.md.`

const W9A_PROMPT = `${RULES}

TASK (W9a: reproduce the status versus version resolver divergence; do not fix it).
Claim to verify: \`callisto status\` resolves changeset package names through PackageId::resolve_unique (crates/callisto-model/src/identity.rs, used in crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/status.rs, around lines 28-60 and 170-210), while \`callisto version\` aggregates through IdentityIndex::resolve_id (crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs and aggregate.rs). Where they differ, a valid secondary native name can be rejected by one and accepted by the other, or an unrelated qualified selector accepted.

Steps
1. Read both resolvers and list every kind of selector a changeset or config can use (bare name, ecosystem-qualified id such as cargo/foo, colon form cargo:foo, a dual-manifest package's secondary native name) and, for each, what each resolver returns on: a unique package, a dual-manifest package whose Cargo and npm names differ, two same-named packages in different ecosystems (promoted), and a name that exists in no package.
2. Reproduce black-box with the real binary: a workspace with a directory holding BOTH a Cargo.toml named for example "native-core" and a package.json named "@org/native-core-js", and a changeset that names the npm name. Run \`callisto status\` and \`callisto version --dry-run\` (or the nearest non-writing equivalent) and record the exact exit codes and messages of each. Do the same for the other selector kinds in your table.
3. Add one test file under crates/callisto-cli/tests/ with one test per divergent selector kind. Tests that currently pass and pin agreement are normal tests. A test that asserts the two commands AGREE but currently fails because they diverge must be marked #[ignore = "status and version resolve this selector differently"] so the suite stays green, and its failure output must be quoted in your report. Do not encode a policy about which side is right.
4. Report: a table of selector kind by resolver by outcome; which divergences are real; which are by design according to docs/specs/workspace.json (read it: does it define display-level matching?); and the smallest change that would make status use the version resolver, without making it.
Scoped verification: cargo test -p callisto-cli --test <your test file> and clippy for callisto-cli only.`

const LANES = [
  { key: 'w10', prompt: W10_PROMPT },
  { key: 'w9b', prompt: W9B_PROMPT },
]

const [lanes, w9a] = await Promise.all([
  pipeline(
    LANES,
    (lane) => agent(lane.prompt, { label: `implement:${lane.key}`, phase: 'Implement', schema: IMPL_SCHEMA, isolation: 'worktree' }),
    (impl, lane) => {
      if (!impl) return null
      if (impl.stopped_early) return { key: lane.key, impl, review: null }
      return agent(
        `You are an independent, adversarial reviewer. Another engineer implemented this task in the worktree ${impl.worktree}. The unmodified base tree (current main) is ${BASE}. Do NOT edit anything in either tree and do NOT run git (it is refused). Compare the two with \`diff -ru\` on the relevant crate src and tests directories (exclude target/), and read the changed files in full.

TASK THEY WERE GIVEN:
${lane.prompt}

THEIR REPORT:
${JSON.stringify(impl, null, 1)}

Find real defects, each with a concrete failing scenario (inputs and state, then the wrong output) and whether you confirmed it by running a test in a scratch copy or only by reading. Probe specifically: a regression for npm or Python manifests or for existing Cargo behavior; other callers or sibling code with the same defect that they did not migrate; public API or constructor changes not recorded; error paths that write a partial file; tests that would pass on the old code (check that each claimed failing-first test really exercises the defect); comments or messages that violate the repository rules (history words, spec IDs, emojis, multi-line comments); anything the tests would not catch. If you can run cargo tests, run the new tests in THEIR worktree (cargo builds there are fine, one at a time) and quote the output; otherwise say you could not. Say plainly if you find nothing significant.`,
        { label: `review:${lane.key}`, phase: 'Review', schema: REVIEW_SCHEMA }
      ).then((review) => ({ key: lane.key, impl, review }))
    }
  ),
  agent(W9A_PROMPT, { label: 'reproduce:w9a', phase: 'Reproduce', schema: IMPL_SCHEMA, isolation: 'worktree' }),
])

const out = {}
for (const lane of lanes.filter(Boolean)) out[lane.key] = lane
log(`lanes completed: ${Object.keys(out).join(', ') || 'none'}; w9a ${w9a ? 'returned' : 'failed'}`)
return { lanes: out, w9a }

